he case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=- 2 x - 2 y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=- 2 x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =- 2 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =- 2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =- 2 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}+\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =4 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - 2 x + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)} \\\\  & =2 \\left(2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x \\right)} - x + \\sin{\\left(2 x \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535725 17592186045443 true] [17592186526418 1080 "- 6 x e^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}} \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)} + 2 e^{- 3 \\sin{\\left(4 x \\right)}}" 13194139535725 17592186045443 true] [17592186526418 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535725 17592186045443 true] [17592186526418 1083 #uuid "7387c28f-4867-5085-b7c6-81bc1baa6e9e" 13194139535725 17592186045443 true] [17592186526419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186526405 13194139535725 17592186045443 true] [17592186526419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186526429 13194139535725 17592186045443 true] [17592186526419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186526443 13194139535725 17592186045443 true] [17592186526419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186526447 13194139535725 17592186045443 true] [17592186526419 1075 #edb/ref 17592186526454 13194139535725 17592186045443 true] [17592186526419 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535725 17592186045443 true] [1759218